To save a JavaScript object to `localStorage`, you first need to serialize it into a string using `JSON.stringify()`. Then, you can store it using `localStorage.setItem()`. To read it back, use `localStorage.getItem()` and deserialize it with `JSON.parse()`.

Here's an example:

```javascript
// Save object to localStorage
const myObject = { name: "Alice", age: 30 };
localStorage.setItem("user", JSON.stringify(myObject));

// Read object from localStorage
const storedObject = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("user"));
console.log(storedObject.name); // Outputs: Alice
```

This allows you to persist data across page reloads or sessions.
